a file date is measured from 2001, not 1970

CFURLCreatePropertyForKey read a stat time and handed it straight to CFDateCreate, which takes a
CFAbsoluteTime. Every date a URL reported was therefore 978307200 seconds late, and an application
reading timeIntervalSince1970 back off it got a date 62 years in the future.

iA Writer records the file date in its own library index, so the application is the instrument:

    before   Item.dateModified = 2766792321.5332756   mtime = 1788485121.5332758   delta 978307200.0
    after    Item.dateModified = 1788485121.5332758   mtime = 1788485121.5332758   delta 0.0000000

The fractional part survives on both sides, which is what makes the delta exact rather than close.

Four keys were wrong the same way: creation, content access, content modification and attribute
modification. Doing the arithmetic in one place also keeps the nanoseconds out of a double that was
already holding 1.8e18, which is past where a double counts by ones.

--- a/NSURL.m
+++ b/NSURL.m
@@ -182,6 +182,14 @@
     return false;
 }
 
+/* stat reports seconds since 1970 and CFDateCreate takes seconds since 2001. */
+static CFDateRef CFDateCreateFromFileTime(time_t seconds, long nanoseconds)
+{
+    CFTimeInterval unix = (CFTimeInterval)seconds + (CFTimeInterval)nanoseconds / (CFTimeInterval)NSEC_PER_SEC;
+
+    return CFDateCreate(kCFAllocatorDefault, unix - kCFAbsoluteTimeIntervalSince1970);
+}
+
 static pthread_mutex_t resInfoLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
 
